{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c4ec","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a1a0b7-0bf0-7f20-bcaf-0661f6003fed"},"sequence":208,"recorded_at":1780531400000412,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"01a1a0b7-123e-7703-a22d-f7a8f557459a","payload_type":"run.terminal.completed","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"run_terminal","command_id":"01a1a0b7-123e-7703-a22d-f7a8f557459a","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"01a1a0b7-123e-7703-a22d-f7a8f557459a"},"terminal":"completed","text":"## Verdict\n\nApprove. No concrete defect requiring a fix was found in the patch.\n\nThe scope is also correct: retaining Shiki JS is appropriate because no WASM migration passed qualification. The sibling `ax-code-diff` pilot should not be integrated: it is unpublished/private and supports duplicate comparison only, not diff preview or hunks.\n\n## Correctness review\n\nThe switch from `marked.lexer(...)` to `new marked.Lexer().blockTokens(...)` is semantically sound for this module’s contract. The only fields consumed from the returned tokens are `type` and `raw`; inline markdown is subsequently parsed by remark from the block text. Therefore, skipping marked’s inline tokenization does not remove syntax handling from the rendering pipeline—it removes a redundant first pass.\n\nUsing a fresh lexer for every call is important and correctly handled. Any undrained inline queue, tokenizer state, or reference-link state remains local to that invocation and is discarded. There is no cross-message or cross-render leakage.\n\nThe CRLF/CR fix addresses the root cause rather than a symptom. Previously, the cache stored the original text while `stablePrefixLength` was derived from marked token raws, which are already LF-normalized. With CRLF input, those coordinate systems diverged and stable content could be sliced incorrectly. After the patch:\n\n- the input is normalized before cache comparison and slicing;\n- the cache stores that normalized text;\n- stable lengths and token raws are expressed in the same coordinates;\n- later appends are checked against the normalized prefix.\n\nThe added split-CRLF case is particularly relevant. A CRLF sequence split between streaming updates is normalized consistently on each call, and the incremental result is compared against from-scratch lexing. Combined with the repeated-prefix CRLF regression test and the reuse-identity assertion, this covers the failure mode that motivated the change.\n\nThe `hasOpenFence` change is behaviorally equivalent for the relevant inputs. Taking the substring after the final `\\n` in the trimmed text produces the same last line as splitting and selecting the final element, including the no-newline case. It avoids constructing an array of all lines while preserving the existing fence-size and closing-fence checks.\n\nMalformed-input behavior remains acceptable. Block-lexer failures still fall back to a single whole-text live block. Reference definitions retain the existing whole-text fallback, so the renderer is not depending on marked’s inline/link resolution from the partially drained lexer state.\n\n## Residual considerations\n\nThere is one maintainability caveat, not a current defect: directly constructing `marked.Lexer` bypasses any future global `marked.use(...)` extensions or non-default marked options that `marked.lexer(...)` might otherwise observe. Under the current stated architecture—default block lexing only, with remark owning inline parsing—that has no effect. It would deserve revisiting only if marked extensions are introduced later.\n\nNo performance conclusion should be drawn from this patch alone. It removes duplicate inline tokenization and avoids a full line split in the fence check, but the added normalization is still linear and remark remains the dominant parser cost. The accurate claim is reduced redundant work, not a demonstrated end-to-end speedup.\n\nThe reported test history is consistent with that assessment: three targeted failures before the fix demonstrate that the new tests capture the regressions, and the 59 passing focused tests provide reasonable coverage for the changed behavior.","reason":null}}
